{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>An unslaughtered carcass and one torn by a predator he shall not eat, to become impure through it</b> — He says that one must be warned against eating an unslaughtered carcass (nevelah) and one torn by a predator (terefah), lest one become impure through it and need to abstain from sacred foods, and not eat of them until one washes one's flesh in water and the sun sets and one becomes pure. And the reason is that since He mentioned the impurity of a person for all his impurities — for He mentioned leprous affliction and flux, encompassing both male and female, and corpse impurity and seminal emission and the creeping thing — He returned to warn concerning sacred foods regarding the impurity of carcasses, and warned about them by way of admonition against eating them. And the reason is that since a person's soul recoils from eating creeping things but does not recoil from an unslaughtered carcass and one torn by a predator, He warned concerning the impurity in what is readily encountered. And the terefah mentioned here is the prey of a lion or bear that killed it in the field, for a living animal does not transmit impurity; for from the moment the animal is torn by the lion it is called terefah, whether alive or after death. And behold, He mentioned here all the impurities in their entirety, for an impure animal, after He said that it has no valid slaughter — as I explained in the section \"And it came to pass on the eighth day\" (Ramban on Leviticus 11:24) — behold, it falls under the category of unslaughtered carcass, and this is the way of its plain sense. And its midrash (Sifra, chapter 4:12–13) likewise warns here concerning sacred foods in a state of impurity, and Scripture speaks of the unslaughtered carcass of a pure bird, which has no impurity of contact or carrying but only the impurity of the place of ingestion, and He forbade it to be eaten in sacred foods. And it is necessary to say \"and one torn by a predator\" — that which has a terefah in its species, this excludes the unslaughtered carcass of an impure bird, which has no terefah in its species, according to the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 22:8). And the impurity of other unslaughtered carcasses, they derived it in the Sifra (chapter 4:4): \"that which touches any creeping thing\" (Leviticus 22:5) — I have only the creeping thing; from where to include the unslaughtered carcass? Scripture says, \"in all\" —"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {0 "The Sifra citations include chapter and paragraph numbers as they appear in the Hebrew source text."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:51.498359706Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}